## Context

Child of ticket 1507 (tracker). The author split the work on 2026-11-10: the
Codex part first, the Pi part by a second raid (ticket 1509, blocked by this
one). `scripts/trace-compact-audit.py` (ticket 1499, PR 2391) walks
`~/.claude/projects` and parses Claude Code's transcript format only; the
shared rules bind Codex to the same ceilings (`skills/route/ceilings.json`
names Sol and Astra), so its count leaves Codex sessions out.

PR 2397 (open on 2026-11-10, another session's) adds the interim line "Claude
Code transcripts only" and two constants, `RUNTIMES_READ` and
`RUNTIMES_NOT_READ`, in the same script. This ticket builds on it once merged
and replaces the constants with what was actually read.

Observed on padme, 2026-11-10, on the 120 most recent `rollout-*.jsonl` files
under `~/.codex/sessions` (3561 files in all, 512 modified in ten days; key
names and numbers only, no transcript content read):

- 2771 `event_msg` records of type `token_count`, none with a null `info`.
  `payload.info.last_token_usage` carries `input_tokens`,
  `cached_input_tokens`, `cache_write_input_tokens`, `output_tokens`,
  `reasoning_output_tokens`, `total_tokens`; `info` also carries
  `total_token_usage` (cumulative) and `model_context_window` (475000 on 2208
  records, 258400 on 563).
- `cached_input_tokens <= input_tokens` on all 2771;
  `total_tokens == input_tokens + output_tokens` on 2767 (4 other);
  `cache_write_input_tokens == 0` on all. This points to `input_tokens`
  already holding the cached part; cause of the 4 exceptions not established.
- `session_meta.payload.source`: `exec` on 99 files, `cli` on 6, a
  `subagent` object on 16 (with `parent_thread_id`); one file has
  `forked_from_id`; 4 `compacted` records.
- `turn_context.payload.model`: `codex-auto-review` 276, `gpt-6.1-sol` 182,
  `gpt-6-luna` 27, `gpt-6-astra` 4, `gpt-6-sol` 2, `gpt-6` 1,
  `openai/gpt-6.1-sol` 1.
- Largest `input_tokens` per file: 449770, 448158, 244700, 239252; the rest
  under 230K.

## Scope

- A reader for Codex rollouts behind the audit's existing session trajectory,
  same definition of context length as ticket 1499: the whole prompt of a turn.
- The seam the Pi reader (ticket 1509) plugs into: a runtime is a corpus root
  plus a reader that yields the trajectories the session report already takes.
  Adding a runtime is then one reader and one entry, with no edit to another
  runtime's reader.
- The audit's table and JSON say which runtimes were read and count sessions
  per runtime. Pi stays named as not read until ticket 1509 lands.
- A runtime whose corpus is absent or unreadable is reported as could not
  look, never as zero sessions.

Out of scope: the Pi reader (ticket 1509); a status line or live display on
Codex; compaction and missed-run cost on Codex (its compaction records and
prices differ: Codex sessions enter the session counts, not the missed-run
dollar figures); a plugin framework for readers.

## Test

On fixture transcripts: a Codex session past its model's ceiling is counted
under Codex; a Codex turn outside the window is not counted; an absent Codex
corpus is reported as could not look while Claude Code is still counted; no
readable corpus at all exits non-zero.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The audit counts Codex main sessions beside Claude Code's, with the
  whole-prompt definition, and its output names each runtime read and each
  one not read
- [ ] A runtime it could not read is reported as such, with a non-zero exit
  only when no corpus at all is readable
- [ ] The Codex `input_tokens` question (does it already include
  `cached_input_tokens`?) is answered in the reader's docstring, with the
  transcript evidence
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Imagine (raid 3429da)

Why now: the author opened the deferred item on 2026-11-10; the ceilings bind
every runtime, and the audit's own table already says a main session is not
known to be an interface session. Codex transcripts say which it is.

Simplest path: leave the Claude Code path and its missed-run dollar figures
as they are. Add a small table of runtimes (name, default corpus root, reader
yielding the `(project, session_id, agent_id, trajectory)` tuples the session
report already takes). The session report runs over the main trajectories of
every readable runtime, each row carrying its runtime. The summary gains, per
runtime, a status (read or could not look), its root and its session counts,
and replaces the two constants of PR 2397. No reader class hierarchy, no
plugin framework, no third-party package: a table of functions.

Points the plan must settle, with the evidence in the code or its docstring:

1. Whole prompt on Codex: `last_token_usage.input_tokens` alone on the probe
   evidence, never the cumulative `total_token_usage`; explain the 4 records
   where `total_tokens != input_tokens + output_tokens`.
2. Session role, from `session_meta.payload.source`: a `subagent` is not a
   main session; `exec` is an unattended run by construction, so it is counted
   as a session and held to the default ceiling; `cli` takes its model's
   ceiling. The output says which rule each count used.
3. Main model: `codex-auto-review` is the most frequent `turn_context` model
   in the sample. The model of a prompt is the one whose request produced
   that `token_count`, or the report names the reviewer as the main model.
4. Forks and resumes (`forked_from_id`, `subagent_history_start_ordinal`,
   `history_mode`): replayed history is not counted twice. Codex token counts
   carry no message id to dedupe on; establish whether a fork's rollout
   replays them.
5. A turn: several `token_count` records per user turn, one per model
   request, and possible re-emissions; a `token_usage_record` line type exists
   in nearly the same number (2764 against 2771). The "30 turns at a level"
   floor counts model requests on Claude Code; count the same thing here.
6. Codex sessions enter the session counts only. Missed runs and their dollar
   figures stay Claude Code only, and the table says so.
7. The window uses each record's own `timestamp`.

8. Corpus root: `~/.codex/archived_sessions/` holds rollouts dated inside
   the window (19 on 2026-11-10), and `$CODEX_HOME` moves the whole tree. The
   reader walks both directories under the Codex home, or the output names
   the archive as not read.
9. Role split: over the 512 rollouts of the last ten days, `source` is `exec`
   on 265, `subagent` on 222 and `cli` on 25. The Codex corpus is mostly
   launched workers, so the table shows the split by role beside the count.
10. The seam's contract, for the Pi reader: a reader yields a session's
    events in conversation order, not file order. Pi records form a tree
    (`id`, `parentId`) with abandoned branches.
11. The output names the host it read: the audit reads the local home only,
    and the harness runs on more than one host.

Blind-Spot pass (2026-11-10, one read-only seat): not clean; points 8 to 11
are its findings. It also found the two downstream readers
(`scripts/trace-hypotheses.py`, `skills/trace-doctor/SKILL.md`) safe as long
as `missed_runs` and `recoverable_usd_upper_bound` keep their Claude Code
meaning, and Codex `cli_version` 0.165.0 to 0.167.0 inside the window, the
`token_count` shape unchecked on the 6 files of 0.165.0. For the author:
whether the count means this host or the fleet (queued in the raid report).

Later review: the tracker's integration review runs the audit on the live
corpora and checks the Codex count by hand against the four files the probe
found above 230K.

## Plan (raid 3429da)

Written by the raid lead from the source; hunt's Plan phase owns the detail.

Base: main with PR 2397 merged (it adds `RUNTIMES_READ`, `RUNTIMES_NOT_READ`
and the JSON keys `corpus_root`, `runtimes_read`, `runtimes_not_read`).

Actions:

1. First test, red for the targeted reason: a Codex fixture root with one
   `cli` session on `gpt-6.1-sol` holding 30 or more in-window `token_count`
   records past 450K, beside a Claude Code fixture root; the audit must count
   one Codex session past its ceiling and leave the Claude Code counts as
   they are.
2. The Codex reader and its corpus walk (sessions and archive, `$CODEX_HOME`).
3. The runtime table and the per-runtime session report; `runtimes_read` and
   `runtimes_not_read` become what was read, Pi staying not read.
4. Could not look per runtime; exit code non-zero only when no corpus is
   readable.
5. The table: runtimes read, sessions per runtime, role split, host, and the
   line that missed runs are Claude Code only.
6. Docstring: the `input_tokens` answer with its evidence.

Tests that would pass on a wrong implementation, and the fix:

- A whole-prompt test whose fixture has `cached_input_tokens == 0` passes
  whether or not the reader adds the cached part. The fixture carries a
  non-zero cached part inside `input_tokens`, sized so that adding it again
  crosses the ceiling and the right reading does not.
- A could-not-look test that only checks the exit code passes if the absent
  runtime is silently dropped. It asserts the runtime is named as not read
  and absent from the session counts, and that the Claude Code counts equal
  those of a run without the Codex root.
- An `exec` session past 230K and under its model's ceiling is counted past
  its ceiling; the same turns in a `cli` session are not. One test, both
  sessions, or the role rule is untested.
- A subagent rollout past the default ceiling is not a session.

Dependencies: PR 2397 merged; none on ticket 1509, which depends on this.

## Feasibility (raid 3429da)

PASS, read on main at a7a5f202 by the raid lead:

- `scripts/trace-compact-audit.py`: `audit_corpus(projects_dir, days,
  min_run, threshold=None, *, since, now, ceilings, metric)` raises
  `CouldNotLook` when the projects dir is absent; `session_report(main_trajs,
  ceilings, metric)` takes `(project, session_id, traj)` 3-tuples filtered
  from the 4-tuples by `agent_id == "main"`; `ceiling_for(model, ceilings)`
  matches a model id by word, so `gpt-6.1-sol` and `openai/gpt-6.1-sol` both
  take the Sol ceiling; `main()` returns 2 on `CouldNotLook`.
- `tests/test_context_length_audit.py` loads the script by path and builds
  fixture transcripts with `_turn` and `_session`; the Codex fixtures follow
  that pattern. `test_cli_missing_projects_dir_is_could_not_look` pins the
  current exit code and changes meaning: an absent Claude Code corpus beside a
  readable Codex one no longer exits non-zero.
- No API key, no network. Corpora are read for key names and numbers only.
- WARN, cross-ticket: PR 2397, this ticket and ticket 1509 edit the same
  script. Order: 2397, then 1508, then 1509; no shared registry beyond the
  runtime table this ticket creates.
